```java
public class Circle {
    private double radius;
    private double origin_x;
    private double origin_y;


    public Circle(double origin_x, double origin_y, double radius) throws IllegalArgumentException {
        if(radius < 0)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Radius can't be negative");
        this.origin_x = origin_x;
        this.origin_y = origin_y;
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    public double area() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }

    public double circumference() {
        return 2 * Math.PI * radius;
    }

    public double distanceFromOrigin() {
        return Math.hypot(origin_x, origin_y);
    }

    public static boolean doCirclesCollide(Circle c1, Circle c2) {
        double distance = Math.hypot(c1.origin_x - c2.origin_x, c1.origin_y - c2.origin_y);
        if (distance > (c1.radius + c2.radius))
            return false;

        if (distance < Math.abs(c1.radius - c2.radius))
            return false;

        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Circle{" +
                "radius=" + radius +
                ", origin_x=" + origin_x +
                ", origin_y=" + origin_y +
                ", area=" + this.area() +
                ", circumference=" + this.circumference() +
                ", distanceFromOrigin=" + this.distanceFromOrigin() +
                "}";
    }
}
```
In this Java program, a class `Circle` is defined with origin coordinates `origin_x and origin_y` and `radius` as data members. The program checks if the radius is negative and throws an `IllegalArgumentException` if the radius is negative. The area, circumference and distance from origin is calculated in the methods `area(), circumference() and distanceFromOrigin ()`. Two circles' collision is checked in `doCirclesCollide() method`, and `toString()` method is overridden for a comprehensive description.

