Below is an example of an Apex trigger which will fire when an Opportunity is created and update the Owner field of the Account related to that Opportunity with the same `OwnerId`.

```Apex
trigger UpdateAccountOwner on Opportunity (after insert) {
    List<Account> accToBeUpdated = new List<Account>();

    for(Opportunity opps : Trigger.new){
        if(opps.AccountId != null){
            Account a = [SELECT  Id, OwnerId FROM Account WHERE Id =:opps.AccountId];
            a.OwnerId = opps.OwnerId;
            accToBeUpdated.add(a);
        }
    }

    if(!accToBeUpdated.isEmpty()){
        update accToBeUpdated;   
    }
}
```
This trigger is an after insert trigger meaning that it fires after the record has been created. 

The trigger loops through all the new Opportunities in trigger.new. For each opportunity, it runs a query to get the Account related to that Opportunity. It then sets the OwnerId of the Account the same as the OwnerId of that Opportunity. 

Finally, all Accounts to be updated are stored in a list and updated with a single DML statement.

Please note, you might want to use a bulkified method to gather all the Opportunity related Account Ids first, then retrieve the Account records in a single query outside of the loop and apply the updates. This way you prevent hitting SOQL governor limits in Salesforce for larger data volume operations.

