import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    List<String> planets = Arrays.asList("Mercury", "Venus", "Earth", "Mars", "Jupiter", "Saturn", "Uranus", "Neptune");

    public List<String> planetsBetween(String planet1, String planet2) {
        int index1 = planets.indexOf(planet1);
        int index2 = planets.indexOf(planet2);

        if (index1 == -1 || index2 == -1) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("One or both of the provided planets are incorrect");
        }

        if (index1 > index2) {
            return planets.subList(index2+1, index1);
        } else {
            return planets.subList(index1+1, index2);
        }
    }
}
```

In the above code, we use the `List.indexOf()` method to find the index of the given planet names in the defined order. We check if any or both of the planet names do not exist in the list by checking if the returned index is -1, in which case we throw an `IllegalArgumentException`.

Then, we return a sublist of the planets between the two given planets (exclusive), taking into account the order of proximity to the sun. The `List.subList()` method returns a view of the portion of this list between the specified `fromIndex`, inclusive, and `toIndex`, exclusive. Therefore, we increase the `fromIndex` by 1 to exclude the `fromIndex` planet. Finally, we check for the order of the planets by comparing `index1` and `index2` and swapping them if necessary.

