Writing out a complete solution with code excerpts for this task can be quite lengthy and complex as it needs a solid understanding of JavaScript and CSS3. However, here's a simplified guideline on how to do it.

1. **HTML setup:**
First, you'll need to set up your HTML structure with elements that you want to apply the parallax effect to. Give them relevant ids or classes so that you can select them later using JavaScript.

```html
<section id="parallax-section">
  <div class="parallax-item" id="item1"></div>
  <div class="parallax-item" id="item2"></div>
</section>
 ```

2. **CSS Setup:**
In your CSS file, you need to make sure you include the necessary styling for your background images to cover the entire div and set fixed positioning, which is needed for a parallax effect. 

```css
.parallax-item {
  /* set other styles like height, width, etc */
  background-attachment: fixed;
  background-position: center;
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
  background-size: cover;
}
```

3. **JavaScript / jQuery setup:**
Now comes the trickier part, using JavaScript or jQuery to create the parallax effect which could be done using either pure JavaScript or jQuery.

```javascript
window.addEventListener('scroll', function(e) {
  const scrolled = window.scrollY;
  const item1 = document.getElementById('item1');
  const item2 = document.getElementById('item2');

  item1.style.transform = 'translateY(' + -(scrolled * 0.2) + 'px)';
  item2.style.transform = 'translateY(' + -(scrolled * 0.5) + 'px)';
});
```

The multiplication factors (0.2 and 0.5 above) are simply "speeds" for the parallax effect. You can adjust them for faster or slower scrolling effect on individual items. 

4. **Responsive/Mobile adaptability:**
To ensure the parallax effect behaves correctly on mobile, you may disable it using a media query in JavaScript to check if the device is mobile or not.

```javascript
if (!(/Mobi/.test(navigator.userAgent))) {
    // Parallax effect code
}
```
The above checks if the user agent string contains 'Mobi', which almost all mobile browsers do, and only runs the parallax effect if it's not there, i.e., on desktop browsers.

5. **Cross Browser Compatibility:**

One of the ways to ensure that our design is compatible is to use the feature detection. Modernizr is a library you can run on your website which adds classes to your html element. It is really useful to see if the visitor's browser supports the CSS properties that you need for parallax.

```html
<script src="modernizr.js"></script>
<script>
  if (!Modernizr.csstransforms3d) {
    // fallback / handle lack of feature
    // could use simpler / static design, or use JS based parallax libraries as fallback
  }
</script>
```

WARNING: Performance of Parallax design could be reduced on devices with less computing power. Also, some users may have disabled the javascript in the browser. Increased load times may happen on websites with large image files because parallax scrolling requires hefty data and resources. 

