The quicksort algorithm is a divide and conquer sorting algorithm that works by selecting a 'pivot' element from the array and partitioning the other elements into two sub-arrays, according to whether they are less than or greater than the pivot.

Here are the steps involved in the quicksort algorithm:

1. Choose an element from the array to be the pivot. This selection can be random or strategic (like always choosing the middle element). The performance of the algorithm can significantly depend on how the pivot is selected.

2. Partitioning: Rearrange the array so that all elements with values less than the pivot come before the pivot, while all elements with values greater than the pivot come after it. After this step, the pivot is in its final position. This is the key process of the quicksort algorithm.

3. Recursion: Apply the above steps recursively to both sub-arrays which are on the left and the right of the pivot element. The recursion continues until the sub-array size becomes zero or one, which is a base case (an array of size zero or one is already sorted).

Pseudo-code for quicksort algorithm:

``` pseudo
function quicksort(array, low, high) 
{
    if (low < high) 
    {
        pi = partition(array, low, high);
        quicksort(array, low, pi - 1); // Before pi
        quicksort(array, pi + 1, high); // After pi
    }
}

function partition(array, low, high) 
{
    pivot = array[high];  
    i = (low - 1) // Index of smaller element

    for (j = low; j <= high- 1; j++) 
    {
        // If current element is smaller than the pivot
        if (array[j] < pivot) 
        {
            i++; // increment index of smaller element
            swap array[i] and array[j]
        }
    }
    swap array[i + 1] and array[high]) 
    return (i + 1); 
}
```

The time complexity of quicksort in the average case is O(n log n), but in the worst case (when the array is already sorted or reverse sorted) it can go up to O(n^2). This is why the pivot selection is crucial. The pivot should be chosen so that it's as close as possible to the median of array elements to get the best performance.

Quicksort is done in-place, meaning it doesn't require any extra storage (apart from the call-stack), which makes it an efficient algorithm for memory usage.

