```swift
class MinStack {
    private var stack: [(val: Int, minVal: Int)] = []

    func push(val: Int) {
        if stack.isEmpty {
            stack.append((val, val))
        } else {
            let minVal = min(val, getMin())
            stack.append((val, minVal))
        }
    }

    func pop() {
        if !stack.isEmpty {
            stack.removeLast()
        }
    }

    func top() -> Int {
        return stack.last?.val ?? 0
    }

    func getMin() -> Int {
        return stack.last?.minVal ?? Int.max
    }
}
```

The `MinStack` class uses a tuple to store both the value and the minimum value at each position in the stack. When pushing a new value, it calculates the minimum between the new value and the current minimum, and stores it along with the value. This allows constant time retrieval of the minimum value at any point. The pop operation simply removes the last element from the stack. The top operation retrieves the value at the top of the stack, and the getMin operation retrieves the minimum value. All operations have constant time complexity.

