You are tasked with implementing a custom awaiter in C#. The awaiter should be able to handle both synchronous and asynchronous operations. Your task is to complete the implementation of the `ValueAwaiter<TResult>` struct, which represents an awaiter that can be created around an existing value or another async operation, represented by either a `Task<TResult>` or an `IAwaiter<TResult>`.

The `ValueAwaiter<TResult>` struct should implement the `IAwaiter<TResult>` interface, which has the following members:
- `bool IsCompleted { get; }`: A property that returns true if the asynchronous operation is completed; otherwise, false.
- `TResult GetResult()`: A method that returns the result of the asynchronous operation.
- `void OnCompleted(Action continuation)`: A method that schedules the continuation action to be invoked when the asynchronous operation completes.

Your task is to complete the implementation of the `ValueAwaiter<TResult>` struct by providing the necessary members and logic to support both synchronous and asynchronous operations.

You should also consider the proper handling of exceptions and synchronization in the implementation.

```csharp
public interface IAwaiter<TResult>
{
    bool IsCompleted { get; }
    TResult GetResult();
    void OnCompleted(Action continuation);
}
```

```csharp
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public interface IAwaiter<TResult>
{
    bool IsCompleted { get; }
    TResult GetResult();
    void OnCompleted(Action continuation);
}

[StructLayout(LayoutKind.Auto)]
public struct ValueAwaiter<TResult> : IAwaiter<TResult>
{
    private readonly Task<TResult> _task;
    private readonly IAwaiter<TResult> _awaiter;
    private readonly TResult _result;

    public ValueAwaiter(Task<TResult> task)
    {
        _task = task;
        _awaiter = null;
        _result = default;
    }

    public ValueAwaiter(IAwaiter<TResult> awaiter)
    {
        _task = null;
        _awaiter = awaiter;
        _result = default;
    }

    public ValueAwaiter(TResult result)
    {
        _task = null;
        _awaiter = null;
        _result = result;
    }

    public bool IsCompleted
    {
        get
        {
            if (_task != null)
            {
                return _task.IsCompleted;
            }
            else if (_awaiter != null)
            {
                return _awaiter.IsCompleted;
            }
            else
            {
                return true; // Synchronous operation, already completed
            }
        }
    }

    public TResult GetResult()
    {
        if (_task != null)
        {
            return _task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        else if (_awaiter != null)
        {
            return _awaiter.GetResult();
        }
        else
        {
            return _result; // Synchronous operation, return the stored result
        }
    }

    public void OnCompleted(Action continuation)
    {
        if (_task != null)
        {
            _task.GetAwaiter().OnCompleted(continuation);
        }
        else if (_awaiter != null)
        {
            _awaiter.OnCompleted(continuation);
        }
        else
        {
            continuation?.Invoke(); // Synchronous operation, invoke the continuation immediately
        }
    }
}
```

This solution provides a complete implementation of the `ValueAwaiter<TResult>` struct, which can handle both synchronous and asynchronous operations. It includes constructors for creating the awaiter around a `Task<TResult>`, an `IAwaiter<TResult>`, or a synchronous result. The `IsCompleted`, `GetResult`, and `OnCompleted` methods are implemented to handle the different scenarios and delegate the operations to the underlying asynchronous or synchronous operations. Additionally, proper null checks and exception handling are included in the implementation.

