}
```

Identify the issues with this code and modify it accordingly. Note that you should also include the capability to determine whether the retrieved URL leads to an HTTPS website or a HTTP website and output a statement if it's HTTPS. Furthermore, the solution should be written in a way that it will function correctly in all modern browsers.

The incorrect code provided is trying to get the url using "document.url", when it should be using "document.URL" because JavaScript is case-sensitive. Furthermore, no error handling mechanism is presented within the code. 

JavaScript exception handling can be performed using a "try/catch/finally" structure. All code is placed within the "try" block. If an error (an exception) is thrown within the "try" block, it is catched within the "catch" block. Irrespective of whether an error is thrown or not, the "finally" block always gets executed.

For checking if a particular URL is "http" or "https", we can write a simple function that inspects the start of the URL string and detects whether it begins with "https".

Below code clears all the issues discussed above:

```javascript
function getURL() {
    try {
        // get the url
        var url = document.URL;

        // First check if url is undefined or null
        if(url === null || url === undefined) {
            throw new Error('URL is not defined/initialized');
        }

        if(/^(http|https):\/\//i.test(url)) {
            // if it is HTTP or HTTPS website
            console.log(url);
            var isSecure = false;
            if(url.substring(0,5) === "https"){
                isSecure = true;
                console.log("This website is secure using HTTPS."); 
            }
            return isSecure;
        } else {
            throw new Error('The URL does not have HTTP or HTTPS prefix');
        }
    } catch (error) {
        console.log('An error occurred: ' + error.message);
        return false;
    } finally {
        console.log('Finished trying to retrieve and validate the URL'); 
    }
}
```
Within the try block, we inspect the URL for the HTTP/HTTPS prefix. If it's not there, we throw an error, which then gets caught in the catch block. The catch block prints out the corresponding error message. 

