Here is a simple JavaScript code snippet that calculates the modulus (remainder) of any two numbers, even with negative values and zero:

```javascript
function modulus(a, b) {
    // Handle edge case where divisor is zero
    if(b === 0) {
      throw new Error("Divisor cannot be zero");
    }
    // Handle negative dividends or divisors
    else {
      var result = a % b;
      return result >= 0 ? result : result + Math.abs(b);
    }
}

console.log(modulus(34, 5)); // prints 4
console.log(modulus(-34, 5)); // prints 1
console.log(modulus(34, -5)); // prints -1
console.log(modulus(-34, -5)); // prints -4
console.log(modulus(34, 0)); // throws error: "Divisor cannot be zero"
```

This code uses the modulus operator `%` to find the remainder of `a` divided by `b`. If `b` is zero, it will throw an error message stating that the divisor cannot be zero. 

However, if the remainder or divisor is negative, `result >= 0 ? result : result + Math.abs(b)` will convert it to a positive remainder by adding the absolute value of the divisor.

