Creating a hand-drawn effect cannot be solely achieved using inline SVG. This is because SVG itself doesn't provide any filters or properties that can convert vector graphic into a hand-drawn sketch. If you are creating an SVG from scratch, you can mimic a hand-drawn look by making the lines wobbly and imperfect. But, converting an existing SVG into a hand-drawn look would need the help of image manipulation tools like Adobe Illustrator, or web libraries like Roughjs.

Roughjs is a light-weight, canvas based, JavaScript library that can be used to create a hand drawn look. 

Here are the steps on how you can add hand-drawn effect using Roughjs:

1. First you need to include the "rough.js" library in your project. You can install it via npm or include it using script tag.

    ```html
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/rough@4.4.1/dist-umd/rough.js"></script>
    ```

2. After including the library, you can use it to give hand-drawn effect to your svg as shown below:

    ```JavaScript
    var rc = rough.svg(document.querySelector('svg'));

    document.querySelector('path').forEach((path) => {
        let d = path.getAttribute('d');
        path.parentNode.replaceChild(rc.path(d), path);
    });
    ```

In the above code, we are selecting the svg and each path in it. Then we are replacing each path with a new path generated by Rough.js. The new path replicates the old one but has a hand-drawn style.

Note that with this approach, the svg cannot be changed dynamically as the transformation is done on page load. If you want to apply the effect dynamically on SVG changes, you would need to apply Roughjs on every change.

