```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LRUCache {
    private final int capacity;
    private LinkedHashMap<Integer, Integer> cache;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new LinkedHashMap<Integer, Integer>(capacity, 0.75f, true) {
            protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry eldest) {
                if (size() > capacity) {
                    return true;
                } else {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        };
    }

    public int get(int key) {
        return cache.getOrDefault(key, -1);
    }

    public void put(int key, int value) {
        cache.put(key, value);
    }

    public void remove(int key) {
        cache.remove(key);
    } 

    public void printCurrentStats() {
        System.out.println("Current Cache Size : " + cache.size());
        System.out.println("Current Cache Keys : " + cache.keySet());
    }
}
```

This LRU cache implementation uses a LinkedHashMap where the entries maintain by order of access from least-recently accessed to most-recently, with the most-recently accessed entry at the head. The capacity argument of the LinkedHashMap is used to adjust the initial capacity. The load factor is maintained to 0.75f to keep a good balance between time and space cost (The default of 0.75 offers a good trade-off between time and space costs). The last boolean argument 'true' denotes that this linked hash map should be kept in access order.

The 'removeEldestEntry()' is overridden in the current implementation, which allows to keep the caching mechanism in place. It will remove the least recently used item when cache size is more than the given capacity.

The 'get' method is used to fetch an entry from cache. The 'put' method is used to insert a key and 'remove' is used to remove a key from cache. 'printCurrentStats' is used to print current size in the cache and all the keys currently being held.

