```java
public class SimpleStack {
    private int[] stackArray;
    private int top;
    private int capacity;

    public SimpleStack(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.stackArray = new int[capacity];
        this.top = -1;
    }

    public void push(int val) {
        if (top < capacity - 1) {
            top++;
            stackArray[top] = val;
        } else {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack overflow");
        }
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (top >= 0) {
            int val = stackArray[top];
            top--;
            return val;
        } else {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("Stack underflow");
        }
    }

    public int peek() {
        if (top >= 0) {
            return stackArray[top];
        } else {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("Stack is empty");
        }
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return top == -1;
    }
}
```

The provided solution implements the `SimpleStack` class with the specified operations using an array as the underlying data structure. The `push` method adds an element to the top of the stack, the `pop` method removes and returns the top element, the `peek` method returns the top element without removing it, and the `isEmpty` method checks if the stack is empty. The solution also includes appropriate error handling for stack overflow, underflow, and empty stack scenarios.

